window.FC = window.FC || {};
FC.story = {
 "scenes": [
  {
   "id": "madrid-1587",
   "title": "",
   "html": "<p>The court of Philip II, king of Spain, had been settled in Madrid since 1561 <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"https://www.redalyc.org/pdf/7370/737080494002.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-key=\"web:https://www.redalyc.org/pdf/7370/737080494002.pdf\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Gloël 2017</a>)</span>, and the king lived in its royal palace, the Alcázar <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"https://www.museodelprado.es/en/learn/encyclopedia/voice/alcazar-de-madrid-real/cd2eb3b7-3aa7-45f9-9ce5-0ed711622f08\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-key=\"web:https://www.museodelprado.es/en/learn/encyclopedia/voice/alcazar-de-madrid-real/cd2eb3b7-3aa7-45f9-9ce5-0ed711622f08\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Morán Turina n.d.</a>)</span>. The king was the source of favor, so people came to court to ask for it <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-millanFilosofiaCortesanaAlonso1996\" data-key=\"millanFilosofiaCortesanaAlonso1996\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Martínez Millán 1996, 465–66</a>)</span>. These petitioners were the <em>pretendientes</em>. What they pressed for was a <em>merced</em>, a grant made by the king's grace, not a wage owed for service, and always scarcer than the men who wanted it <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-luceroIdeaTrabajoFilosofia2019\" data-key=\"luceroIdeaTrabajoFilosofia2019\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Lucero 2019c, 823, 829</a>)</span>. Philip II's courtiers, as Fernando Collar de Cáceres puts it after the historian Geoffrey Parker, had among their obsessions boredom and promotion (<q class=\"sq\" lang=\"es\">aburrimiento y promoción</q>) <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-decaceresTableroItalianoFilosofia2009\" data-key=\"decaceresTableroItalianoFilosofia2009\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Collar de Cáceres 2009, 82</a>)</span>; the book and the game answered both. The game's title runs along the top of the sheet: <em lang=\"es\">Filosofia cortesana de Alonso de Barros</em> <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite cite-board\" href=\"about.html#about-board\" data-key=\"ref:board\" aria-expanded=\"false\">board</a>)</span>.</p>"
  },
  {
   "id": "barros",
   "title": "",
   "html": "<p>The man who made it signed himself <em lang=\"es\">criado del Rey</em>, the king's servant <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite cite-board\" href=\"about.html#about-board\" data-key=\"ref:board\" aria-expanded=\"false\">board</a>)</span> <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-sanchezEdicionesAntiguasFilosofia2016oct16\" data-key=\"sanchezEdicionesAntiguasFilosofia2016oct16\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Lucero Sánchez 2016, 174, 179</a>)</span>. Barros, a Segovian, took over his dead father's post of <em>aposentador</em>, one of the officials who found lodgings for the court, in 1563, and died in it in 1604 <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-decaceresTableroItalianoFilosofia2009\" data-key=\"decaceresTableroItalianoFilosofia2009\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Collar de Cáceres 2009, 82</a>; <a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-luceroDEDICATORIAFILOSOFIACORTESANA2019\" data-key=\"luceroDEDICATORIAFILOSOFIACORTESANA2019\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Lucero 2019b, 34</a>)</span>. As his modern editor tells the story, the post never contented him: from the early 1580s he petitioned the king for more in written memorials <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-luceroDEDICATORIAFILOSOFIACORTESANA2019\" data-key=\"luceroDEDICATORIAFILOSOFIACORTESANA2019\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Lucero 2019b, 34</a>)</span>. The book is dedicated to his patron, Mateo Vázquez de Leca, secretary to Philip II <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-luceroDEDICATORIAFILOSOFIACORTESANA2019\" data-key=\"luceroDEDICATORIAFILOSOFIACORTESANA2019\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Lucero 2019b, 33–35</a>; <a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-sanchezFILOSOFIACORTESANAALONSO\" data-key=\"sanchezFILOSOFIACORTESANAALONSO\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Lucero Sánchez 2020, 22</a>)</span>. Vázquez had made his own way up: E. M. Wilson records that he “was born in Algiers, where his mother was a Christian captive” <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-wilsonCervantesItemEmmanuel1968\" data-key=\"wilsonCervantesItemEmmanuel1968\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Wilson 1968, 365</a>)</span>. Ernesto Lucero, who edited the book in 2019, sums up Barros's method: “Alonso de Barros conceived of writing as a service at court” (<q class=\"sq\" lang=\"es\">Alonso de Barros concibió la escritura como un servicio cortesano.</q>) <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-luceroDEDICATORIAFILOSOFIACORTESANA2019\" data-key=\"luceroDEDICATORIAFILOSOFIACORTESANA2019\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Lucero 2019b, 33, 35 n. 3</a>; <a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-sanchezFILOSOFIACORTESANAALONSO\" data-key=\"sanchezFILOSOFIACORTESANAALONSO\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Lucero Sánchez 2020, 18</a>)</span>.</p>"
  },
  {
   "id": "what-for",
   "title": "",
   "html": "<p>Barros says what he meant in his preface to the reader: “my intention has been nothing but to show, in jest and in play, the earnest truths and misfortunes that follow long petitioning” (<q class=\"sq\" lang=\"es\">mi intento no ha sido sino mostrar entre burla y juego las veras y desventuras que siguen a una larga pretensión</q>) <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-debarrosFilosofiaCortesana1587\" data-key=\"debarrosFilosofiaCortesana1587\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Barros 1587, Madrigal ed., PDF 3</a>; <a class=\"cite\" href=\"text.html#pdf17\" data-key=\"barrosFilosofiaCortesanaMoralizada1588\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Barros 1588, sig. A6r</a>)</span>. His dedication calls the book a teaching for anyone who seeks advancement, so that they “may know the beginnings, the means and the ends by which human petitions travel, and where they come to rest” (<q class=\"sq\" lang=\"es\">sepan los principios, los medios y los fines por do caminan y vienen a parar las pretensiones humanas</q>) <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-luceroDEDICATORIAFILOSOFIACORTESANA2019\" data-key=\"luceroDEDICATORIAFILOSOFIACORTESANA2019\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Lucero 2019b, 35</a>; <a class=\"cite\" href=\"text.html#pdf19\" data-key=\"barrosFilosofiaCortesanaMoralizada1588\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Barros 1588, sig. A7r</a>)</span>. His friend the novelist Mateo Alemán wrote in 1598 that it could as well be called “the disillusioning of office-seekers” (<q class=\"sq\" lang=\"es\">desengaño de pretensores</q>) <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-sanchezEdicionesAntiguasFilosofia2016oct16\" data-key=\"sanchezEdicionesAntiguasFilosofia2016oct16\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Lucero Sánchez 2016, 170–71</a>)</span>. The board also says two things at once: its verses praise hard work, while its dice hand out luck. Lucero draws the conclusion: “Then Fortune always wins” (<q class=\"sq\" lang=\"es\">Entonces, Fortuna siempre vence.</q>) <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-luceroIdeaTrabajoFilosofia2019\" data-key=\"luceroIdeaTrabajoFilosofia2019\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Lucero 2019c, 829</a>)</span>.</p>"
  },
  {
   "id": "goose-arrives",
   "title": "",
   "html": "<p>The game he borrowed was the Game of the Goose, a race. Players throw two dice and move their markers along a spiral of 63 squares; some squares help, some send a player back, and the first to land exactly on the last square takes the pot <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-luceroFilosofiaCortesanaJuego2020\" data-key=\"luceroFilosofiaCortesanaJuego2020\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Lucero 2020, 122</a>; <a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-woodChancingItPrint2019\" data-key=\"woodChancingItPrint2019\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Wood 2019, 458</a>)</span>. E. M. Wilson, who described Barros's game in English in 1968, compared it to Snakes and Ladders and to Monopoly <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-wilsonCervantesItemEmmanuel1968\" data-key=\"wilsonCervantesItemEmmanuel1968\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Wilson 1968, 367</a>)</span>. The goose game was new at the Spanish court; a writer of 1617 said it came from Florence, a gift of the grand duke of Tuscany to Philip II <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-sevilleImportanceTextPrinted2026\" data-key=\"sevilleImportanceTextPrinted2026\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Seville 2026, 69</a>)</span>. In 1585 the king's jester complained in a letter that the prince and the infanta had won forty escudos from him at this devilish game, “played with two dice” (<q class=\"sq\" lang=\"es\">que se juega con dos dados</q> <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-infantesPinturaQueSe2010\" data-key=\"infantesPinturaQueSe2010\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Infantes 2010, 135</a>)</span>), and that “the king likes to see me stung” (<q class=\"sq\" lang=\"es\">gusta el rey de verme picado</q>) <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-infantesPinturaQueSe2010\" data-key=\"infantesPinturaQueSe2010\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Infantes 2010, 134–35</a>)</span>. The bridge outlined here, square 15, is Barros's version of the goose game's Bridge, where a player paid a toll and moved on <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-luceroFilosofiaCortesanaJuego2021juin20\" data-key=\"luceroFilosofiaCortesanaJuego2021juin20\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Lucero 2021, 144</a>; <a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-woodChancingItPrint2019\" data-key=\"woodChancingItPrint2019\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Wood 2019, 458</a>)</span>.</p>"
  },
  {
   "id": "moral-map",
   "title": "",
   "html": "<p>Barros kept the race of the goose game (63 squares on a spiral, an exact throw to finish) and changed its meaning <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-luceroFilosofiaCortesanaJuego2020\" data-key=\"luceroFilosofiaCortesanaJuego2020\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Lucero 2020, 125</a>)</span>. The geese became yoked oxen, the square of Labor <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-luceroFilosofiaCortesanaJuego2020\" data-key=\"luceroFilosofiaCortesanaJuego2020\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Lucero 2020, 124</a>)</span>. Death became the death of one's patron <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-luceroFilosofiaCortesanaJuego2020\" data-key=\"luceroFilosofiaCortesanaJuego2020\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Lucero 2020, 124</a>)</span>. The other squares became the chances of a petition at court: hope, flattery, false friends, a change of ministers, the patron's death, poverty <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-luceroFilosofiaCortesanaJuego2021juin20\" data-key=\"luceroFilosofiaCortesanaJuego2021juin20\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Lucero 2021, 135, 149</a>)</span>. For Barros the 63 squares are the years of life that a petition consumes <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-luceroFilosofiaCortesanaJuego2021juin20\" data-key=\"luceroFilosofiaCortesanaJuego2021juin20\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Lucero 2021, 135</a>)</span>. Lucero calls the game “the first metaphorical, themed version of the goose game on record” (<q class=\"sq\" lang=\"es\">la primera tematización metafórica del juego de la oca de que tenemos constancia</q>) <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-luceroFilosofiaCortesanaJuego2020\" data-key=\"luceroFilosofiaCortesanaJuego2020\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Lucero 2020, 117</a>)</span>.</p>"
  },
  {
   "id": "sheet-and-book",
   "title": "",
   "html": "<p>It came in two parts. The royal license to print, of 9 February 1587, says that Barros had composed “a picture entitled <em lang=\"es\">Filosofía cortesana</em>, with a number of different figures and verses, contained on a large sheet” (<q class=\"sq\" lang=\"es\">una pintura intitulada Filosofía cortesana, con ciertas diferencias de figuras y letras, que se contiene en un pliego grande</q>) <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-luceroTableroJuego15882019\" data-key=\"luceroTableroJuego15882019\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Lucero 2019a, 198</a>)</span>. He had also, it says, “moralized it in a separate account” (<q class=\"sq\" lang=\"es\">la habéis moralizado en una relación aparte</q>) <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-luceroTableroJuego15882019\" data-key=\"luceroTableroJuego15882019\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Lucero 2019a, 198</a>)</span>: he had written a moral explanation of it. The sheet was the board; the little book gave the rules and explained each picture <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-luceroTableroJuego15882019\" data-key=\"luceroTableroJuego15882019\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Lucero 2019a, 197–98</a>)</span>. Barros's second edition says the sheet traveled apart from the book, to be spread on a table or pasted onto one <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-debarrosFilosofiaCortesana1587\" data-key=\"debarrosFilosofiaCortesana1587\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Barros 1587, Madrigal ed., PDF 11</a>)</span>. Kelli Wood shows that in Rome such printed sheets were gambled on by the nobility and in common drinking-houses alike, and she explains why so few survive: they “were pasted down on tables in taverns, gambled on, and then pasted over when they were worn out” <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-woodChancingItPrint2019\" data-key=\"woodChancingItPrint2019\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Wood 2019, 451, 469</a>)</span>. E. M. Wilson put it plainly: <q class=\"sq\" lang=\"en\">You improve yourself morally while you gamble.</q> <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-wilsonCervantesItemEmmanuel1968\" data-key=\"wilsonCervantesItemEmmanuel1968\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Wilson 1968, 369</a>)</span></p>"
  },
  {
   "id": "reading-a-square",
   "title": "",
   "html": "<p>Each square with a picture works like an emblem, the picture-and-verse form popular in the period. Lucero maps the parts: the square's name is the motto, the picture is the image, and the verses under it, together with Barros's prose in the book, are the explanation <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-luceroFilosofiaCortesanaJuego2020\" data-key=\"luceroFilosofiaCortesanaJuego2020\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Lucero 2020, 117</a>)</span>. Take square 26. The heading reads <em lang=\"it\">Il priuato</em>, the Favorite <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite cite-board\" href=\"about.html#about-board\" data-key=\"ref:board\" aria-expanded=\"false\">board</a>)</span>. A man offers something to another at a doorway <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite cite-board\" href=\"about.html#about-board\" data-key=\"ref:board\" aria-expanded=\"false\">board</a>)</span>; Barros says it is the first fruits of the petitioner's estate, offered in return for a hearing <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-decaceresTableroItalianoFilosofia2009\" data-key=\"decaceresTableroItalianoFilosofia2009\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Collar de Cáceres 2009, 94</a>)</span>. The Spanish verse below it, in English: “Before you ask another's hand, / fill yours, or empty-handed stand” <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite cite-board\" href=\"about.html#about-board\" data-key=\"ref:board\" aria-expanded=\"false\">board</a>)</span>. A small label above says <em>Paga</em>: pay <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite cite-board\" href=\"about.html#about-board\" data-key=\"ref:board\" aria-expanded=\"false\">board</a>)</span> <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-decaceresTableroItalianoFilosofia2009\" data-key=\"decaceresTableroItalianoFilosofia2009\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Collar de Cáceres 2009, 94</a>)</span>.</p>"
  },
  {
   "id": "three-editions",
   "title": "",
   "html": "<p>The book was printed three times in little more than a year, which Lucero takes as a sign that it was popular: twice in Madrid in 1587, the second time with additions, among them a worked example of play and a sonnet by Cervantes, and once in Naples in 1588 <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-sanchezEdicionesAntiguasFilosofia2016oct16\" data-key=\"sanchezEdicionesAntiguasFilosofia2016oct16\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Lucero Sánchez 2016, 177, 179, 191</a>; <a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-luceroDEDICATORIAFILOSOFIACORTESANA2019\" data-key=\"luceroDEDICATORIAFILOSOFIACORTESANA2019\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Lucero 2019b, 38–40, 47</a>)</span>. One copy of each edition is known, and no board for either Madrid edition; when you play, you can switch between the three versions <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-sanchezEdicionesAntiguasFilosofia2016oct16\" data-key=\"sanchezEdicionesAntiguasFilosofia2016oct16\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Lucero Sánchez 2016, 176–77, 180</a>; <a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-luceroTableroJuego15882019\" data-key=\"luceroTableroJuego15882019\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Lucero 2019a, 202</a>)</span>.</p>"
  },
  {
   "id": "naples-cartaro",
   "title": "",
   "html": "<p>The board that survives was engraved in Naples in 1588 by Mario Cartaro, a printmaker from Viterbo, active first in Rome, who went to Naples in 1586 to work on its maps <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite cite-board\" href=\"about.html#about-board\" data-key=\"ref:board\" aria-expanded=\"false\">board</a>)</span> <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-woodChancingItPrint2019\" data-key=\"woodChancingItPrint2019\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Wood 2019, 462</a>; <a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-sevilleImportanceTextPrinted2026\" data-key=\"sevilleImportanceTextPrinted2026\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Seville 2026, 70</a>)</span>. Naples then belonged to Philip II's crown and was governed for him by a viceroy, the “His Excellency” whose privilege, a ten-year right to print the sheet in the Kingdom of Naples, is engraved at its foot <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite cite-board\" href=\"about.html#about-board\" data-key=\"ref:board\" aria-expanded=\"false\">board</a>)</span> <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-luceroTableroJuego15882019\" data-key=\"luceroTableroJuego15882019\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Lucero 2019a, 202, 203 n. 14</a>; <a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-GameGooseLargest\" data-key=\"GameGooseLargest\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Ciompi &amp; Seville, giochidelloca.it</a>)</span>. Its verses are in Italian and Spanish; Lucero thinks the bilingual board was meant for the Spaniards living in Naples and for the local courtiers <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-decaceresTableroItalianoFilosofia2009\" data-key=\"decaceresTableroItalianoFilosofia2009\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Collar de Cáceres 2009, 86</a>; <a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-luceroTableroJuego15882019\" data-key=\"luceroTableroJuego15882019\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Lucero 2019a, 203 n. 14</a>)</span>. It is a single sheet, etched and engraved, 531 × 404 mm <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-woodChancingItPrint2019\" data-key=\"woodChancingItPrint2019\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Wood 2019, 463</a>)</span>. The only copy recorded in the sources we cite is in the British Museum (1869,0410.2463.+), which bought it in 1869 through the dealer Edward Daniell, from the collection of the Marquis of Salamanca <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-britishMuseumRecord2012\" data-key=\"britishMuseumRecord2012\" aria-expanded=\"false\">British Museum 2012</a>; <a class=\"cite\" href=\"https://www.britishmuseum.org/collection/object/P_1869-0410-2463-\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-key=\"web:https://www.britishmuseum.org/collection/object/P_1869-0410-2463-\" aria-expanded=\"false\">British Museum n.d.</a>)</span>. Scholars recognized what it was in 2009 and 2010 <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-infantesPinturaQueSe2010\" data-key=\"infantesPinturaQueSe2010\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Infantes 2010, 130, 133</a>)</span>.</p>"
  },
  {
   "id": "the-pot",
   "title": "",
   "html": "<p>Money was part of the game. In the second Madrid edition each player puts one stake, a sum agreed before play, into the pot; players pay again at certain squares and each time they are sent back, and the one who lands exactly on 63 takes everything <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-debarrosFilosofiaCortesana1587\" data-key=\"debarrosFilosofiaCortesana1587\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Barros 1587, Madrigal ed., PDF 11–12</a>)</span>. Lucero reads the pot as the court's favors, which “are limited and not enough for everyone” (<q class=\"sq\" lang=\"es\">son limitados y no alcanzan para todos</q>) <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-luceroFilosofiaCortesanaJuego2021juin20\" data-key=\"luceroFilosofiaCortesanaJuego2021juin20\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Lucero 2021, 141</a>)</span>. The winner's square shows a palm, and beside it stands a man who has caught a fish but left a shoe behind: nothing is won without cost <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-decaceresTableroItalianoFilosofia2009\" data-key=\"decaceresTableroItalianoFilosofia2009\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Collar de Cáceres 2009, 97</a>; <a class=\"cite cite-board\" href=\"about.html#about-board\" data-key=\"ref:board\" aria-expanded=\"false\">board</a>)</span>.</p>"
  },
  {
   "id": "cervantes",
   "title": "",
   "html": "<p>Miguel de Cervantes, later the author of <em>Don Quixote</em>, wrote a sonnet for the second edition while he was trying to win Mateo Vázquez's favor; he never won it <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-sanchezEdicionesAntiguasFilosofia2016oct16\" data-key=\"sanchezEdicionesAntiguasFilosofia2016oct16\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Lucero Sánchez 2016, 180–81 and n. 41</a>)</span>. The sonnet praises a book that joins pleasure and profit in a cheerful game <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-wilsonCervantesItemEmmanuel1968\" data-key=\"wilsonCervantesItemEmmanuel1968\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Wilson 1968, 366</a>)</span>. It pictures the reader as a sailor: “whoever sails the mad gulf of the sea of petitions will at once see the thread through the courtly labyrinth” (<q class=\"sq\" lang=\"es\">El que navega por el golfo insano/ del mar de pretensiones vera al punto/ del cortesano laberintio el hilo</q>) <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-bidwell-steinerFilosofiaCortesanaAlfonso2024\" data-key=\"bidwell-steinerFilosofiaCortesanaAlfonso2024\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Bidwell-Steiner 2024, 197</a>)</span>. At the center of the board lies the Sea of Suffering, with its verse: “Who seeks advancement must endure, / as death for all the born is sure” <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite cite-board\" href=\"about.html#about-board\" data-key=\"ref:board\" aria-expanded=\"false\">board</a>)</span> <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-decaceresTableroItalianoFilosofia2009\" data-key=\"decaceresTableroItalianoFilosofia2009\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Collar de Cáceres 2009, 99</a>)</span>.</p>"
  },
  {
   "id": "barros-board",
   "title": "",
   "html": "<p>Barros played his own game for real. In 1582 he was passed over for an accounting post in Guatemala, and in 1583 he was refused the assayership of the Segovia mint <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-decaceresTableroItalianoFilosofia2009\" data-key=\"decaceresTableroItalianoFilosofia2009\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Collar de Cáceres 2009, 82 and n. 10</a>; <a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-millanFilosofiaCortesanaAlonso1996\" data-key=\"millanFilosofiaCortesanaAlonso1996\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Martínez Millán 1996, 463 n. 9</a>)</span>. In 1587, weeks after this book was approved, he asked for a revenue office at Silos, and the king wrote on the file: “Let me know why it has been vacant so long, whether I have been consulted on it before, and whether others ask for it” (<q class=\"sq\" lang=\"es\">Avíseme cómo ha tanto tiempo que está vaca, y si se me ha consultado otras veces, y si la piden otros</q>) <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-luceroDEDICATORIAFILOSOFIACORTESANA2019\" data-key=\"luceroDEDICATORIAFILOSOFIACORTESANA2019\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Lucero 2019b, 35 n. 6</a>)</span>. He got the post <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-luceroDEDICATORIAFILOSOFIACORTESANA2019\" data-key=\"luceroDEDICATORIAFILOSOFIACORTESANA2019\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Lucero 2019b, 35–36</a>)</span>. Then his patrons died: Mateo Vázquez in 1591, García de Loaysa in 1599 <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-luceroDedicatoriaProverbiosMorales2019\" data-key=\"luceroDedicatoriaProverbiosMorales2019\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Lucero 2019d, 109 n. 21, 112</a>)</span>. Lucero writes that the Death of the Patron “sends Barros back to the starting square of his own board” (<q class=\"sq\" lang=\"es\">devuelve a Barros a la casilla de salida de su tablero de la Filosofía cortesana</q>) <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-luceroDedicatoriaProverbiosMorales2019\" data-key=\"luceroDedicatoriaProverbiosMorales2019\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Lucero 2019d, 120</a>)</span>. In 1600, under the new king, he was refused leave to pass his post to a man outside his family <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-luceroDedicatoriaProverbiosMorales2019\" data-key=\"luceroDedicatoriaProverbiosMorales2019\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Lucero 2019d, 119 and n. 48</a>)</span>. He died in 1604, still in the post he had inherited from his father <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-decaceresTableroItalianoFilosofia2009\" data-key=\"decaceresTableroItalianoFilosofia2009\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Collar de Cáceres 2009, 82, 100 n. 11</a>; <a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-luceroDEDICATORIAFILOSOFIACORTESANA2019\" data-key=\"luceroDEDICATORIAFILOSOFIACORTESANA2019\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Lucero 2019b, 34</a>)</span>.</p>"
  },
  {
   "id": "enter",
   "title": "",
   "html": "<p>The way in is a gate <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite cite-board\" href=\"about.html#about-board\" data-key=\"ref:board\" aria-expanded=\"false\">board</a>)</span>. Above it a swan stands on a skull and blows a trumpet that says “Know thyself”; between the pillars is written “Look to the end” <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite cite-board\" href=\"about.html#about-board\" data-key=\"ref:board\" aria-expanded=\"false\">board</a>)</span> <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-decaceresTableroItalianoFilosofia2009\" data-key=\"decaceresTableroItalianoFilosofia2009\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Collar de Cáceres 2009, 93</a>)</span>. Barros explains that the beginner enters by the Gate of Opinion, deceived by their own good opinion of themselves, and struts like the peacock on the first square until time tells them to look at their feet <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite\" href=\"about.html#bib-decaceresTableroItalianoFilosofia2009\" data-key=\"decaceresTableroItalianoFilosofia2009\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Collar de Cáceres 2009, 93</a>)</span>. Its verse: “Reason looks down to the feet; / Opinion, to the wheel's conceit” <span class=\"cites\">(<a class=\"cite cite-board\" href=\"about.html#about-board\" data-key=\"ref:board\" aria-expanded=\"false\">board</a>)</span>.</p>"
  }
 ]
};
